<!-- PAGE 3: CASE STUDIES 1 & 2 -->
<div class="page inner" style="padding-bottom:9mm;">
  <div class="ph">
    <span class="ph-name">Anastasiya Lupashko</span>
    <span class="ph-label">Selected Work</span>
    <span class="ph-num">03</span>
  </div>
  <div class="pc">
    <p class="sec-label">Case Studies</p>
    <h2 class="sec-title">Understanding what drives the numbers</h2>

    <div class="case">
      <div class="case-left">
        <span class="case-method">Longitudinal Qualitative + Behavioural Analytics</span>
        <span class="case-title">How trust builds with a new financial platform, and where it quietly breaks down</span>
        <div class="case-minis">
          <div>
            <span class="mini-num">Multi-wave</span>
            <span class="mini-label">Same cohort tracked over months</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="mini-num">+24%</span>
            <span class="mini-label">Journey completion after insight-led changes</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="case-right">
        <p class="case-body">
          Acquisition metrics were healthy. The problem was that customers who looked engaged after onboarding
          were quietly disengaging weeks later, before the behaviours that predict long-term retention had formed.
          Standard usability testing told us where people stopped in a flow. It could not tell us why their
          confidence was eroding in the weeks between sessions.
        </p>
        <p class="case-body">
          Designed and ran a longitudinal programme tracking the same cohort across multiple months through
          diary studies and repeat in-depth interviews, mapping how trust and confidence developed or
          deteriorated over time. Cross-referenced with behavioural analytics to identify the moments where
          the lived experience diverged from what the platform data suggested was happening.
        </p>
        <p class="case-body">
          Surfaced an accumulation effect: not any single friction point but a pattern of small, unresolved
          uncertainties that compounded over time. Findings directly changed product investment priorities
          and contributed to a 24% improvement in journey completion. The methodology became a standing
          part of the research calendar.
        </p>
        <div class="case-tags">
          <span class="tag">Longitudinal design</span>
          <span class="tag">Diary studies</span>
          <span class="tag">IDIs</span>
          <span class="tag">Behavioural analytics</span>
          <span class="tag">Thematic analysis</span>
          <span class="tag">Opal AI synthesis</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="case">
      <div class="case-left">
        <span class="case-method">Primary Survey + Segmentation + Qual Follow-up</span>
        <span class="case-title">Reaching the customer segments that standard research methods could not find</span>
        <div class="case-minis">
          <div>
            <span class="mini-num">+5%</span>
            <span class="mini-label">Conversion improvement driven by insight</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="mini-num">3+</span>
            <span class="mini-label">Distinct barrier profiles identified</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="case-right">
        <p class="case-body">
          Marketing teams knew certain customer segments were not converting at the rate the acquisition
          economics required. Existing research told them nothing about why: disengaged customers
          don't respond to standard recruitment panels, and the funnel data described the behaviour
          without explaining it.
        </p>
        <p class="case-body">
          Built a primary survey programme from scratch using alternative recruitment channels designed
          specifically to reach disengaged and hard-to-find segments. Adapted question design for
          low-engagement respondents, ran segmentation analysis to surface distinct barrier profiles
          and followed up with qualitative interviews to explain the patterns the survey had revealed.
          Linked findings back to funnel and cohort data to test whether the barriers identified in
          research matched observed drop-off patterns.
        </p>
        <p class="case-body">
          Three distinct barrier profiles emerged, each requiring a different marketing and product
          response. Changes driven by the insight contributed to a 5% improvement in conversion and
          the programme became a standing element of the quarterly insight calendar.
        </p>
        <div class="case-tags">
          <span class="tag">Survey design</span>
          <span class="tag">Segmentation</span>
          <span class="tag">Funnel analysis</span>
          <span class="tag">Cohort data</span>
          <span class="tag">Qual follow-up</span>
          <span class="tag">Recruitment strategy</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
