<!-- PAGE 4: CASE STUDY 3 + CAPABILITIES -->
<div class="page inner" style="padding-bottom:9mm;">
  <div class="ph">
    <span class="ph-name">Anastasiya Lupashko</span>
    <span class="ph-label">Work and Expertise</span>
    <span class="ph-num">04</span>
  </div>
  <div class="pc">

    <div class="case" style="margin-bottom:7mm;">
      <div class="case-left">
        <span class="case-method">Experimentation Programme</span>
        <span class="case-title">Running an insight-fed experimentation programme that moved the numbers</span>
        <div class="case-minis">
          <div>
            <span class="mini-num">75% to 51%</span>
            <span class="mini-label">Homepage bounce rate improvement</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="mini-num">3.17% to 8.44%</span>
            <span class="mini-label">CTA clickthrough improvement</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="case-right">
        <p class="case-body">
          The business had run experiments before. The problem was that test ideas were generated from
          opinions rather than evidence, win rates were low, and the programme had no mechanism for
          learning from failures. Fixing it required not just better test design but a different
          relationship between research and experimentation.
        </p>
        <p class="case-body">
          Built and ran an end-to-end experimentation programme fed continuously by qualitative and
          quantitative research. Qualitative insight generated the hypotheses; behavioural analytics
          identified where to test them; A/B and multivariate experiments validated or disproved
          them at scale. When tests failed, the finding went back into the research loop rather than
          being discarded. When tests won, the qualitative context explained why and informed
          the next round of iteration.
        </p>
        <p class="case-body">
          Over the programme's lifetime: homepage bounce rate from 75% to 51%, CTA clickthrough
          from 3.17% to 8.44%, scroll depth from 15% to 26%, contributing to a 24% increase in
          successful journey completion. Presented results and strategic implications at C-suite
          level on a regular basis.
        </p>
        <div class="case-tags">
          <span class="tag">Hypothesis development</span>
          <span class="tag">A/B testing</span>
          <span class="tag">Multivariate testing</span>
          <span class="tag">Funnel analysis</span>
